WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-AU

00:00:01.360 --> 00:00:06.840
When Raygun performed at the Olympic Games,&nbsp;
her unique approach to breakdancing was pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:13.320
divisive. Some loved it. Some hated it. It felt&nbsp;
like everyone was talking about it. There were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:18.720
claims that Raygun had manipulated the selection&nbsp;
process, that she only got to the Olympics because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:24.320
her husband was a judge, and that she got&nbsp;
zero points in the competition on purpose.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:30.280
This led some upset fans to create a petition&nbsp;
on Change.org. But within days, it was taken&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.280 --> 00:00:34.240
down for breaking Change.org’s&nbsp;
guidelines against harassment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:37.290
bullying or spreading false information.&nbsp;
Because well...&nbsp;

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:46.400
But it can get more serious than that.
In the UK, the spread of false information led&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:52.720
to violence when people on social media started&nbsp;
making false claims about the identity of someone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:58.880
behind a horrific stabbing. Rioters took to the&nbsp;
streets, blocking roads, destroying buildings and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:04.640
targeting communities they thought were to blame.
But again, this outrage wasn't based on facts. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:11.480
was fuelled by misinformation and experts&nbsp;
say misinformation is a growing problem.&nbsp;

00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:15.680
And I think the reason for that is&nbsp;
the technology that makes it very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:20.280
easy for any of us to be a publisher.
Barbara McQuade is interested in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:24.560
ways that misinformation threatens&nbsp;
democracy and national security.&nbsp;

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:30.800
Never before in history has it been possible for&nbsp;
one person to send a message, a false message,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:36.240
that can reach millions of people in an instant.
Misinformation is playing a big role in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:39.880
US politics, particularly in the&nbsp;
lead up to another big election.&nbsp;

00:01:39.880 --> 00:01:44.320
I think it's going to be politics in the news&nbsp;
every day until the election. I'm sure there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:50.120
will be disinformation in the, in the news.
Disinformation is similar to misinformation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.120 --> 00:01:54.360
Both terms refer to false&nbsp;
information, but the difference&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:59.920
is in the intention of the person sharing it.
See, misinformation is information that is wrong,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:04.000
but the person sharing it doesn't&nbsp;
know that. Disinformation is false&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:08.560
information that is deliberately shared&nbsp;
to trick or manipulate people. A figure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:14.320
often linked to disinformation is Donald Trump.
They're eating the dogs, the people that came in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:18.840
they're eating the cats. They're&nbsp;
eating, they're eating the pets.&nbsp;

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:23.480
You know, he didn't invent it. Certainly,&nbsp;
disinformation, propaganda, psyops,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.480 --> 00:02:28.200
all these things have been around for centuries.&nbsp;
But Donald Trump has demonstrated that he's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:33.720
willing to lie really about anything to suit&nbsp;
his purposes, and he also is someone who takes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:39.360
advantage of what is called the liar's dividend.
The liar's dividend is something that becomes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:44.760
possible in a world with lots of misinformation&nbsp;
and disinformation. See, when we all know that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:50.320
politicians can tell lies and that audio&nbsp;
and images and even video can be faked,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:55.520
it becomes possible to point at real&nbsp;
news and real facts and call them fake.&nbsp;

00:02:55.520 --> 00:03:00.680
In other words, if lots of people lie,&nbsp;
it becomes easy to call everyone a liar.&nbsp;

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:04.920
And nobody lies like her. She's&nbsp;
a liar. She makes up crap.&nbsp;

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:10.040
Because of the existence of false information,&nbsp;
he is able to capitalise on this liar's dividend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.040 --> 00:03:15.960
and accuse people of fake news or using&nbsp;
falsehoods when in fact, things are true.&nbsp;

00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:20.440
This makes it even harder for all of us to&nbsp;
separate the fact from the fiction. And with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.440 --> 00:03:25.920
US politics overflowing with misinformation, from&nbsp;
conspiracy theories about the shooting at Trump's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:29.760
rally, to questioning Kamala Harris'&nbsp;
claims of having worked at McDonald's,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:36.800
it's a busy time for fact checkers.
AI has definitely made sort of verifying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:42.400
images, videos, audio, and even who you're&nbsp;
speaking to online a lot more difficult.&nbsp;

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:46.840
Matt Martino tracks and investigates&nbsp;
